# SRG-APP-000119-AU-000110  -  CAT II
# The Central Log Server must protect audit information from unauthorized
# modification.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations, then kept
# by hand. Edit this file to change how this requirement is assessed here;
# it is read at run time. Regenerating refreshes the benchmark text in place
# and leaves the criteria, the determination and any verification block as
# they are.

id:              SRG-APP-000119-AU-000110
group:           V-221915
severity:        medium
cat:             CAT II
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       machine
confidence:      derived
revised:         yes
cci:             CCI-000163
control_rev5:    AU-9

contribution:    Indexed data cannot be edited through Splunk; the only in-product path
                 that changes what a search returns is the delete command, which needs both
                 the delete_by_keyword capability and an index named in the role's
                 deleteIndexesAllowed list, so the application's share is to keep that path
                 off the general-purpose roles and away from the internal indexes.

governs:         authorize.conf - Used to create roles.

check:           authorize.conf [role_user] delete_by_keyword absent
check:           authorize.conf [role_power] delete_by_keyword absent
check:           authorize.conf [role_can_delete] deleteIndexesAllowed not_matches (^|;)[[:space:]]*_
# --- vendor verification: generated, do not edit by hand
corrected:       authorize.conf [role_<roleName>] deleteIndexesAllowed matches tls1\.2 -> authorize.conf [role_can_delete] deleteIndexesAllowed not_matches (^|;)[[:space:]]*_
vendor:          authorize.conf [role_<roleName>] deleteIndexesAllowed https://help.splunk.com/en/data-management/splunk-enterprise-admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.3-configuration-file-reference/authorize.conf
# --- end vendor verification

external:        Write access to the index bucket files on disk - SPLK-CL-000190 implements
                 this with splunk-user ownership and mode 700 at the operating system layer
external:        Which roles the organization authorizes to run the delete command

citation:        Two corrections. delete_by_keyword absent read over every role stanza
                 fails a stock install: the shipped role_can_delete holds it
                 (authorize.conf:243) and exists for exactly that purpose, and neither the
                 check text nor SPLK-CL-000190 directs its removal. It also cannot be read
                 over arbitrary roles at all, because capabilities are inherited through
                 importRoles (authorize.conf.spec:73-74, 79-84), so a role holding the
                 capability by inheritance has no line to read. Pinned to the two shipped
                 general-purpose roles the condition passes stock and fails a real grant.
                 deleteIndexesAllowed not_in *|_* repeats the wildcard inversion: the
                 shipped role_can_delete value is "*" (authorize.conf:247), which per
                 spec:312 and spec:284-288 cannot reach the internal indexes, so the
                 condition failed stock, while "*;_*" would have passed a literal not_in.
finding:         A general-purpose role holds delete_by_keyword, or a role's
                 deleteIndexesAllowed list reaches the internal indexes, so the records in
                 the audit trail can be masked from search by an account that is not
                 privileged.
